{
  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize a controlled experiment: **context-gated receptive fields within NextItNet**. Benefits remain hypotheses; no validation improvement has been measured.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Users reject broad relevance when recent behavior indicates a specific purpose, motivating adaptive context weighting. (2) Older, isolated interactions persist too strongly, motivating explicit tests of historical influence. (3) Electronics has 12.27% sampled ranking errors and a −0.2595 tenth-percentile margin, identifying confusing pairs without establishing validation HR@10. (4) Collapse scores of 0.5071–0.5234 imply near-zero average cosine similarity; global dispersion regularization has weaker diagnostic support.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) [NextItNet](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163) supplies causal dilated residual convolutions, preserving the required family. (2) [Selective Kernel Networks](https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06586) directly precedes input-conditioned scale selection, reducing originality claims; its evidence concerns images. (3) [MARS](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03718) motivates adaptive recency aggregation but uses a timestamp-aware post-encoder mechanism. (4) [ICSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318) motivates subsequence intent supervision; [sampled-softmax analysis](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf) motivates stronger negative competition. Neither establishes gains here.\n\nThese insights define three directions: adaptive context scope, stronger ranking supervision, and subsequence consistency. The conceptual framework is:\n\n| Direction | Mechanism | Diagnostic target | Main uncertainty |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Context scope | Gate causal dilation paths | Stale interests; broad relevance | Contextualized paths retain older dependencies |\n| Ranking supervision | Uniform multi-negative softmax | Weak lower-tail margins | Unobserved positives |\n| Intent consistency | Match overlapping suffix representations | Unstable current preferences | May suppress genuine shifts |\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are prospective: O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment; higher D means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Gated receptive fields | 4 | 6 | 7 | 6 | 5 |\n| Uniform multi-negative softmax | 2 | 6 | 4 | 9 | 2 |\n| Suffix consistency auxiliary loss | 4 | 6 | 7 | 5 | 5 |\n\nGating offers adaptive weighting but has established precedents; user alignment is plausible but cannot establish compatibility; margin gains are unproven; implementation requires causal and predictor checks. Multi-negative softmax directly targets errors and is simple, but is conventional, risks false negatives, and weakly addresses stale interests. Suffix consistency targets coherent preferences and offers an auxiliary-learning direction, but novelty is limited, alignment may erase real shifts, and paired sampling adds complexity.\n\n**Selected experiment.** Jointly train short/original-dilation paths with a scalar causal gate, retaining the baseline objective. Explicit targets are stale-history persistence, broad matches displacing current-purpose matches, and Electronics’ weak separation. Gating contextualized states controls transformations; it cannot guarantee a history cutoff because inputs, gates and residuals inherit older dependencies. Test historical influence rather than claiming removal.\n\nPredeclare baseline, fixed-weight and conditioned-gate comparisons, capacity accounting, checkpoint selection and budget. Track saturation, gradients and history-length dependence; gate permutation is an intervention, not another deployed predictor. Select using unchanged macro validation HR@10. Report paired user-level uncertainty when available, recognizing selection bias and fixed-candidate scope. Compatibility and duplication remain unresolved where necessary facts are unavailable.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add scalar context-dependent gates between short- and original-dilation causal convolution paths inside the supplied corrected NextItNet. Both paths are jointly trained components of one network, with exactly one independently trained model/checkpoint loaded per category. Interpret the mechanism as adaptive transformation weighting, not a guaranteed cutoff of older history.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale isolated interactions and broadly relevant recommendations displacing specific recent-purpose matches. Adaptive scale weighting explicitly targets both, although success requires behavioral evidence. Electronics' 12.27% sampled wrong-rank rate and negative tenth-percentile margin provide a model-facing target. These observations justify an experiment, not an expected HR@10 improvement. Uniform multi-negative softmax remains a simpler subsequent alternative if gating fails; changing both simultaneously would obscure attribution.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the supplied corrected NextItNet. Retain embeddings, hidden width, residual ordering, normalization, objective, optimizer, routing and sampling initially. Record the inherited configuration verbatim, including convolution variant, kernel sizes, dilation schedule, dropout and optimizer settings; these were not supplied in the report and must not be invented. Modify temporal convolutions with original dilation d>1: compute parallel dilation-1 and dilation-d paths with the same input/output dimensions and kernel size. Leave dilation-1 layers unchanged. Combine branch preactivations before the baseline's subsequent normalization/activation. Use a positionwise scalar sigmoid gate computed from the sublayer input, initialized with zero weights and bias -2, favoring the original path. Copy the baseline convolution initialization to the original path; initialize the added path with the baseline initialization rule. Train all parameters jointly. Do not combine independently fitted predictors.\n\nCount every temporal convolution when documenting receptive fields: for stride-one layers, the maximum span is 1 + sum((k_l-1)*d_l); residual blocks may contain multiple convolutions, schedules accumulate across repetitions, and span does not guarantee every offset is reachable. A short branch operating on contextualized states still inherits their older dependencies. If strict bounded history is later tested, derive the branch from raw embeddings within each permitted window and bound its gates and bypasses as well; that is a separate architectural experiment.\n\nUse explicit left padding of (k-1)*d with valid causal convolution, or verify equivalent baseline behavior. Apply channel-only LayerNorm to B×L×C; avoid training normalization that aggregates across timesteps. Keep the padding embedding zero and mask invalid positions after biased convolutions, affine normalization and residual updates before subsequent temporal mixing. Mask padded losses. Verify causality by perturbing future inputs and checking earlier features in deterministic evaluation mode. Test claimed dependency bounds by perturbing inputs outside those bounds. The proposed contextualized gate has no strict short-window invariance claim.\n\nPreserve log2feats(sequence), item_emb and predict_score(features, items), including existing tensor conventions. For left padding, gather the largest nonzero-token index, normally L-1; never use count minus one. Preserve baseline handling of empty histories explicitly. Update the embedded predictor writer, checkpoint architecture configuration and state loading together. Verify that a freshly loaded generated predictor reproduces trained-model scores on identical inputs. Each category loads exactly one selected checkpoint.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Probe the actual view.model through ModelView using valid training positions from (None, sequence, positive, negative). Return measured metrics and definitions; retain margin diagnostics and optionally add gate entropy, saturation, branch gradients and gate dependence on history length. Unavailable category-based breakdowns must be marked unavailable, not fabricated. Only public histories, timestamps, item titles and descriptions are allowed; ratings, reviews, stores, prices, categories and simulator traits are unavailable. Title similarity cannot establish physical compatibility or replacement need.\n\nPredeclare a small comparison matrix: corrected baseline, identical two-path network with fixed gate sigmoid(-2), and conditioned gate. Fixed and conditioned variants isolate adaptation with nearly identical capacity; disclose extra parameters relative to baseline, and use a matched-capacity ungated control only if budget permits. Gate freezing/permutation and inference-only suffix truncations are diagnostic interventions on the same fitted network. Retrained truncation controls, if affordable, are separate trials because inference-only truncation creates distribution shift. Record every attempted configuration and actual GPU time within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget; do not promise a trial count without runtime evidence. Do not inspect other trials or reuse their checkpoints.\n\nKeep preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, at most 200 epochs, exactly 200 batches per epoch, single-GPU execution, four-category routing, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Preserve the baseline checkpoint-selection procedure and validation cadence, recording them before comparison; select configurations by unchanged host macro validation HR@10. Keep the host's 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negative candidates and evaluation seed 42 fixed. No test labels, evaluator changes, extra data or pretrained weights.\n\nWhen host outputs expose per-user binary hits, bootstrap paired differences by resampling users within category and averaging the four category differences equally; report 95% intervals, category differences and discordant hit counts. Otherwise state that paired uncertainty is unavailable. One hit changes category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125 percentage points. These intervals condition on fitted models and fixed candidates, omit training/candidate variability, and do not correct validation-selection optimism. Treat searched gains as exploratory; sampled margins and sampled HR do not establish full-catalog gains. User-facing benefits require the supplied qualitative assessment process, including separate tracking of uncertain identity and explicit mismatch. No training or code execution was performed in this proposal.",
    "pseudocode": "Record inherited baseline configuration, selection rule, comparison matrix and compute budget.\nFor each permitted configuration and each of the four categories:\n    set the prescribed seeds; instantiate one corrected NextItNet\n    preserve baseline sublayer ordering and objective\n    for each original temporal convolution with dilation d > 1:\n        replace its preactivation with:\n            short = masked_causal_conv(input, dilation=1)\n            original = masked_causal_conv(input, dilation=d)\n            gate = sigmoid(positionwise_linear(input))\n            mixed = gate * short + (1 - gate) * original\n        continue baseline normalization, activation and residual processing\n        reapply validity masks before further temporal mixing\n    train jointly for at most 200 epochs, with 200 batches per epoch\n    use unchanged next-item loss on valid positions\n    preserve the predeclared baseline checkpoint-selection procedure\n    save one selected model with complete architecture configuration\nSelect the configuration using unchanged host macro validation HR@10.\nAt prediction:\n    load one selected model/checkpoint for the routed category\n    compute log2feats(sequence)\n    gather the largest nonpadding index, handling empty histories explicitly\n    score candidates through predict_score(features, items)\nVerify causality, padding behavior and generated-predictor score consistency.\nReport measured margins and gate diagnostics separately from validation HR@10.\nIf available, compute paired user-level uncertainty; disclose selection bias.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Provides a focused NextItNet adaptation linking adaptive scale weighting to the supplied behavioral and margin diagnoses.",
      "negative": "Input-conditioned receptive-field selection has direct precedent in Selective Kernel Networks, and adaptive recency aggregation is established; novelty of this exact adaptation is unverified."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Controlled interventions can reveal when adaptive convolution scales help distinguish current purpose from persistent interests.",
      "negative": "Contextualized branches cannot enforce historical forgetting, gate shortcuts may explain apparent gains, and missing compatibility facts remain unresolved."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "The architecture change remains localized and preserves the existing family, objective and scoring interfaces.",
      "negative": "Receptive-field accounting, causal masking, capacity controls, gate diagnostics and generated-predictor consistency add meaningful implementation and verification work."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet: A Simple Convolutional Generative Network for Next Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes residual dilated causal convolutions for autoregressive sequential recommendation.",
        "Supplies the backbone and sampled-softmax motivation retained in the proposed research directions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish benefits of the proposed context-dependent scale gate.",
        "Dilation choices alone do not establish a strict recent-history dependency bound."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Original NextItNet convolution and residual-block implementation",
      "link": "https://github.com/fajieyuan/WSDM2019-nextitnet/blob/master/ops.py",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides concrete causal padding and residual-block implementation details.",
        "Shows why receptive-field accounting must count every convolution, including d and 2d convolutions in the two-convolution variant."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied corrected host baseline may use a different block variant; its actual configuration must be recorded."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Selective Kernel Networks",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06586",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a direct precedent for input-conditioned selection among branches with different receptive fields."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Evidence concerns image classification rather than causal sequential recommendation.",
        "Does not establish novelty or effectiveness of this NextItNet adaptation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MARS: Multi-rate Aggregation of Recency Signals for Sequential Recommendation across Sparse and Dense Regimes",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03718",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses user-conditioned temporal decay summaries, diversity regularization and adaptive aggregation.",
        "Motivates testing context-dependent recency weighting."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "It is a timestamp-aware post-encoder aggregator, not a gated dilated-convolution architecture.",
        "Reported Transformer/Mamba results do not demonstrate transfer to NextItNet or this benchmark.",
        "The selected experiment uses positional convolution scales rather than timestamp decay."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "ICSRec",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates intent learning through contrastive supervision over subsequences."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not prove that short-context preferences should dominate every recommendation.",
        "Its auxiliary supervision introduces additional choices beyond the selected experiment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Wu et al., TOIS 2024: sampled-softmax analysis",
      "link": "https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Analyzes how sampled softmax emphasizes high-scoring negatives.",
        "Motivates the alternative uniform multi-negative training experiment."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied empirical evidence concerns collaborative-filtering models.",
        "Stronger sampled margins alone do not demonstrate improved host validation HR@10."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains why conclusions from sampled rankings may differ from full-catalog evaluation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not replace the required pinned evaluator or justify changing its candidates."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/beta/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains selection-induced optimism from repeatedly optimizing against finite validation data.",
        "Supports predeclaring comparisons and recording all attempted configurations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Ordinary paired bootstrap intervals after configuration selection do not correct the selection bias."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch LayerNorm documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.modules.normalization.LayerNorm.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Specifies normalization over trailing dimensions, allowing per-timestep channel normalization with LayerNorm(C)."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Correct normalization alone does not establish causality; convolution padding, gates, bypasses and masks also require verification."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
